# Cross-nursery circular wakeup — the case-A flat-scheduler fix (M:N / --parallel ONLY).
#
# `inner` spawns an implicit (nested) nursery; the OUTER `parallel:` spawns sibling O and then calls
# inner(a, b). O recvs a → sends b; inner's spawned fiber sends a → recvs b. The cross-nursery
# circular wait used to fault `deadlock` because the inner nursery's inline owner drained only its
# own private queue and could never RUN the outer sibling O. With the M:N flat scheduler (one global
# MnSched, scope-scoped owner stop), the inline owner's OS thread drains the GLOBAL queue, so it runs
# O, which unblocks I.
#
#   chezzi run --parallel examples/parallel_cross_nursery_circular.chz   # → I got 1 / O got 1 / done
#
# Output ORDER note: O and I live in DIFFERENT nurseries (O in main's outer, I in inner's), and each
# nursery flushes its buffered task output at its OWN join (inner's join < main's join) — so the order
# is "I got 1" then "O got 1", NOT the single-nursery case-C order. Both tasks complete; the runtime
# wake order is O-then-I, but the buffered Decision-F flush is per-nursery-join.
#
# NOTE: `--parallel` is an accepted no-op alias — M:N is the only engine, so plain `run` succeeds too.
# See docs/concurrency-tier-d.md "Open / deferred".
fn inner(a: Channel[int], b: Channel[int]):
    spawn:
        a.send(1)
        y := b.recv()
        print("I got {y}")

fn main():
    a := Channel[int]()
    b := Channel[int]()
    parallel:                        # OUTER nursery
        spawn:                       # O: child of OUTER
            x := a.recv()
            b.send(x)
            print("O got {x}")
        inner(a, b)                  # inner's spawn → NESTED nursery, joins at fn return

    print("done")

main()
